00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.660
A covered call ETF strategy lets investors

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.640
collect premium income on holdings they already own

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.240
by selling out of the money calls each month.

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.720
Today's subdued Wall Street futures

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.220
and focus on the Fed make income generation more attractive

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.740
than pure directional bets,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.700
especially when volatility compresses.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.680
Investors can implement this on platforms

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.980
like Questrade or Interactive Brokers

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:48.860
by selecting a broad equity ETF such as QQQ or XIU,

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.440
then sell monthly calls at the 30 delta strike.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.040
The premium is collected regardless of modest upside.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.940
This approach has historically performed best

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:01.800
in range bound or mildly bullish markets

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.860
where the underlying drift sideways.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:07.460
It can deliver eight to 12% annualized yield

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.580
on top of any capital appreciation.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.520
The main risk is opportunity cost

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.260
if the underlying rally sharply above the strike

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:16.280
capping gains.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:19.280
Always size the position so the covered calls

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.260
represent no more than 20 to 30% of total equity exposure

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:25.280
to preserve upside participation.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.900
That income tactic works best

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.780
when your overall allocation avoids over concentration

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.440
in any single market.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.540
Imagine you allocated 40% of your TFSA to Canadian equities.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.200
Last year when the TSX lagged global markets

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.620
by 12 percentage points.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:43.720
Your order filled cleanly,

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:46.760
but the real cost showed up in mist compounding

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:48.660
from US tech and healthcare names

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.620
that drove the NASDAQ higher.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:52.560
The mechanism is simple.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.060
Canada represents roughly 3% of global market capitalization

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.100
yet many DIY investors hold 30 to 50% in domestic stocks

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.980
because of the dividend tax credit and familiarity.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.680
What most retail investors do not realize

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.000
is that their job, home, and pension

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.800
already embed a massive Canada exposure.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.740
So the portfolio ends up triple exposed

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.040
to financials and energy,

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:20.520
which together make up half the TSX.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.240
The professionals always check geographic revenue breakdown

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.240
before adding another Canadian name.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:28.760
The biggest mistake with home bias

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:31.180
is assuming familiarity reduces risk.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:34.940
Instead, always measure your true economic exposure

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:39.640
across borders and cap Canadian equities at 15 to 25%.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:41.960
Unless you have a specific tactical reason.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.440
That kind of geographic discipline

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:47.080
pairs naturally with disciplined position sizing

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:48.260
on individual names.

